[Event "01. Promote safely (1/8)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/PP6/k7/8/K7/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "01. Promote safely (1/8)"]
[LineDescription "Before you promote, check that the new queen is safe and that the black king still has a move."]

{[%csl Ga8,Gb8,Ba7,Bb7,Bb6] Welcome! Two connected pawns stand side by side, or
one diagonally behind the other, so they can protect each other. Every line ends
when a pawn becomes a safe queen: the black king cannot take it, and the black
king still has a move (otherwise it is stalemate, only a draw). First the
finish: which pawn goes, and when? Both pawns can become a queen right now, but
only one of the new queens is safe. Green: the queening squares. Blue: the
squares the black king can step to.} 1. a8=Q+ {Queen! The b-pawn protects the
new queen. With a queen, the win is easy from here. b8=Q would have been
stalemate.} *

[Event "01. Promote safely (2/8)"]
[Site "Endgame Classroom"]
[White "?"]
[Black "?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/5PP1/7k/8/6K1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[LineName "01. Promote safely (2/8)"]
[LineDescription "Before you promote, check that the new queen is safe and that the black king still has a move."]

{[%csl Gf8,Gg8,Bg7,Bh7,Bg6] One move to a queen. Which one is safe?} 1. f8=Q
{Queen! The g-pawn protects the new queen. With a queen, the win is easy from
here. g8=Q would have been stalemate.} *

